<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hawaiʻi Ponoʻī</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">words <a href="/years/1874">1874</a>; adopted as national anthem <a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Hawaiʻi Ponoʻī&quot; (&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>'s Own&quot;) was written in 1874 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">King David</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">Kalākaua</a> of the Hawaiian Kingdom. The music is by Captain Henri Berger, then the king's royal bandmaster. Its melody recalls &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot; and the Prussian anthem &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heil-dir-im-siegerkranz">Heil dir im Siegerkranz</a>&quot;. In 1876 it was adopted as the national anthem of the Hawaiian Kingdom, replacing &quot;He Mele Lāhui Hawaiʻi&quot;, a composition by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani">Liliʻuokalani</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Hawaiʻi State Legislature considered it for the official state song in 1967, and the Hawaiʻi State Constitutional Convention again in 1978, but both times &quot;<b>Hawaiʻi Ponoʻī</b>&quot; was chosen instead, a song written by King David Kalākaua and composed by Henri Berger, master of the Royal Hawaiian Band.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hawaii-aloha">Hawaiʻi Aloha</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Hawaiʻi Ponoʻī</b>&quot; (&quot;Hawaii's Own&quot;) was written in 1874 by King David Kalākaua of the Hawaiian Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hawaii-ponoi">Hawaiʻi Ponoʻī</a></li></ul></section>
